om Lemma 2 (any slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and not hitting another point of \\(T_3\\); for instance):  \n\n  \\[\n  L_2:\\; y-1 = 2(x-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L_3:\\; y-1 = 3(x-2) \\qquad\\text{(slope }3).\n  \\]\n\n* Remaining diagonal line: \\(D_4:\\; x+y=4\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n\\((1,1)\\in L_2\\), \\((2,1)\\in L_3\\), \\((1,2)\\in D_4\\).  \nThus all required points are covered and exactly two of the three lines are sunny, confirming the construction for \\((3,2)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nA collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, because each line is either sunny or not. Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.\n\nConversely, the construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
